.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESS-LIST-SECRETS 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ess-list-secrets \- list stored secrets
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ess list-secrets
[\fB\-\-prefix\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fITEXT\fR]
[\fB\-\-page\-size\fR|\fB\-s\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-page\-index\fR|\fB\-i\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-sort\-column\fR|\fB\-c\fR \fICOLUMN\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Lists the secrets of the caller's account and namespace with their
descriptions, versions, the key each is encrypted under and when each
was last rotated.
.PP
Never their values. The response has nowhere to put one, so no amount
of paging or filtering here can produce a value \-
.BR euclid-cli-ess-get-secret (1)
is the only action that does, one secret at a time.
.PP
Sorting by
.I rotated
puts the stalest first, which is what answers which passwords nobody
has changed since they were first written down.
.PP
A principal deployed with particular secrets sees exactly those, so an
application can list what it may read without being refused.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-prefix " " \fITEXT\fR
Only secrets whose name starts with this. Defaults to all.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-page\-size " " \fIN\fR
Page size. Defaults to \fI10\fR.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-page\-index " " \fIN\fR
Zero-based page index. Defaults to \fI0\fR.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-sort\-column " " \fICOLUMN\fR
Sort column: \fIname\fR, \fIern\fR or \fIrotated\fR. Defaults to
\fIname\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The secrets and the total count are printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
The server could not be reached, or it rejected the request.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ess (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-get-secret (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-create-secret (1)
